    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_fund_aggregating_data.s06",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.group_by"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Per-category summaries",
      "title": "Splitting rows into groups with GROUP BY",
      "lede": "A single AVG gives one number for all orders combined. GROUP BY lets you compute that average separately for each customer or region.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "GROUP BY groups together rows that share the same values in the listed columns, combining each group into one output row. Any aggregate function in the SELECT list is then computed separately within each group. `SELECT customer_id, AVG(delivery_time_minutes) AS avg_time FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id` returns one row per customer_id, each showing that customer's own average delivery time.",
          "role": "mental_model",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.group_by"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.group_by#f1"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.group_by#tb1",
              "sql.group_by#tb2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "When a table is grouped, any column not listed in GROUP BY cannot appear in the SELECT list except inside an aggregate function. `SELECT customer_id, order_id, AVG(delivery_time_minutes) FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id` would fail because order_id is neither grouped nor aggregated — each customer group contains several different order_ids, so SQL cannot pick one.",
          "role": "misconception",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.group_by"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.group_by#f2"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.group_by#tb3"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
