    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_fund_aggregating_data.s10",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.having"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Filtering groups",
      "title": "Filtering aggregated groups with HAVING",
      "lede": "WHERE filters raw rows before grouping. To filter on an aggregate result — keeping only customers whose average delivery time is high — you need HAVING.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "HAVING filters out groups after GROUP BY has combined rows and computed aggregates. It acts on groups rather than raw rows, much like WHERE acts on rows. `SELECT customer_id, AVG(delivery_time_minutes) AS avg_time FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id HAVING AVG(delivery_time_minutes) > 30` keeps only the customer groups whose average delivery time exceeds 30 minutes.",
          "role": "definition",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.having"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.having#f1"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.having#tb1",
              "sql.having#tb2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "Expressions in HAVING can refer to grouped columns and to aggregate expressions computed over the group. You can use both in the same clause: `HAVING customer_id > 1 AND AVG(delivery_time_minutes) > 30` mixes a grouped column and an aggregate. WHERE and HAVING can also appear in the same query — WHERE removes raw rows before grouping, HAVING then filters the grouped result.",
          "role": "mechanism",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.having"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.having#f2"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.having#tb3"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "process_flow",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_aggregating_data.s10.pf",
          "title": "WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING: the filtering pipeline",
          "direction": "LR",
          "nodes": [
            {
              "id": "n_raw",
              "label": "Raw rows",
              "kind": "start"
            },
            {
