      "title": "Aggregating data with GROUP BY",
      "lede": "Three tools that turn rows into summaries.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "Aggregate functions — COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX — compute one value from many rows. The shape is always the same: many row values in, one summary value out.",
          "role": "takeaway",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.aggregate_functions"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.aggregate_functions#f1"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.aggregate_functions#tb4"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "GROUP BY answers 'per X' questions: list the grouping column, and each aggregate is computed once per bucket instead of once overall.",
          "role": "takeaway",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.group_by"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.group_by#f1"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.group_by#tb4"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "HAVING filters groups after aggregation, just as WHERE filters rows before grouping. Use WHERE to narrow raw rows, HAVING to narrow the grouped result.",
          "role": "takeaway",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.having"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.having#f1"
            ],
