      "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
      "title": "Every SELECT column must be grouped or aggregated",
      "lede": "When GROUP BY is present, SQL enforces a strict rule about which columns can appear in SELECT.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "When a table is grouped, a column not listed in GROUP BY cannot appear in the SELECT list except inside an aggregate function. Each customer group contains several order_ids, so SQL cannot pick one to show — the query `SELECT customer_id, order_id, AVG(delivery_time_minutes) FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id` fails. Fix it by removing order_id or wrapping it in an aggregate such as COUNT(order_id).",
          "role": "misconception",
