      "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
      "title": "HAVING expressions can reference grouped columns and aggregates",
      "lede": "HAVING is more flexible than WHERE — it can test both the grouping column and any aggregate.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "text": "Expressions in HAVING can refer to grouped columns and to aggregate expressions computed over the group. For example, `HAVING customer_id > 1 AND AVG(delivery_time_minutes) > 30` tests a grouped column (customer_id) and an aggregate (AVG) in the same clause. WHERE cannot reference aggregates at all — that is why HAVING exists.",
          "role": "mechanism",
